Description: Fits and compares four flexible families of continuous
    distributions, with the Johnson family as the default. Pearson curves
    described by Pearson (1895) <doi:10.1098/rsta.1895.0010> are fitted by
    four-moment matching through 'PearsonDS'. The unbounded (SU), lognormal (SL) and bounded (SB) translation
    systems of Johnson (1949) <doi:10.1093/biomet/36.1-2.149> are fitted by
    profiled numerical maximum likelihood, and Burr Type III and Type XII
    distributions of Burr (1942) <doi:10.1214/aoms/1177731607>, see also
    Tadikamalla (1980) <doi:10.2307/1402945>, by numerical maximum likelihood.
    The generalized lambda distribution of Ramberg and Schmeiser (1974)
    <doi:10.1145/360827.360840> is used in the parameterization of Freimer,
    Kollia, Mudholkar and Lin (1988) <doi:10.1080/03610928808829820> and is
    fitted by quantile matching with numerical inversion of its quantile
    function. Candidates are ranked by descriptive distances between fitted and
    empirical cumulative distribution functions. Johnson marginal
    transformations to a common normal scale support descriptive dependence
    analysis without implying joint normality on the original scale. The
    package also provides numeric data validation with row-level audits,
    density, distribution and quantile evaluation, and separate plotting
    functions. Count data are treated as continuous approximations. The
    methodology and its application to mobile application usage data are
    described in Karakaya Demir, Purutçuoğlu and Bursalı (2026), accepted
    at the 3rd International Conference on Advanced Technology in Electronic
    and Electrical Engineering (ICATEEE 2026).
